---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPost from '../../components/BlogPost.astro';

// The getStaticPaths function returns an array of page routes, and all of the pages at those routes will use the same template defined in the file.
/*
If you need information to construct the page routes, write it inside getStaticPaths().
To receive information in the HTML template of a page route, write it outside getStaticPaths().
*/
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const allPosts = Object.values(import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', {eager: true}));
  const uniqueTags = [...new Set(allPosts.map((post: any) => post.frontmatter.tags).flat())];

  return uniqueTags.map((tag) => {
    const filteredPosts = allPosts.filter((post: any) => post.frontmatter.tags.includes(tag));
    return {
      params: { tag },
      props: { posts: filteredPosts }, // its the posts set here that are used as props
    };
  });
}

const { tag } = Astro.params;
const { posts } = Astro.props;

---
<BaseLayout pageTitle={tag}>
  <p>Posts tagged with {tag}</p>
  <ul>
    {posts.map((post: any) => <BlogPost url={post.url} title={post.frontmatter.title} />)}
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>

<!--

/*
In src/pages/tags/[tag].astro, the filtering happens inside getStaticPaths in two passes:

Load all markdown posts
allPosts is built from import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', { eager: true }).
That gives an array of post modules, each with frontmatter (including tags).
Build one page per unique tag
uniqueTags collects every tag from every post:
map each post to post.frontmatter.tags
flatten into one list
remove duplicates with Set
For each tag, create filteredPosts
In uniqueTags.map(tag => { ... }), this line does the transformation:
filteredPosts = allPosts.filter(post => post.frontmatter.tags.includes(tag))
So filteredPosts contains only posts whose tags array includes that specific tag.
Return route data Astro uses at build time
For each tag, getStaticPaths returns:
params: { tag } so the URL is generated for that tag
props: { posts: filteredPosts } so only matching posts are passed to that page instance
Then outside getStaticPaths:

const { tag } = Astro.params gets the current URL tag
const { posts } = Astro.props gets the already-filtered list for that tag
So posts is not filtered later in the template. It is pre-filtered in getStaticPaths and injected as props for each generated tag page.
*/


-->